window.FIELD_CHAPTER_HTML["book-1-full-book"] = "<h2>A Comprehensive Handbook for the AI Era</h2>\n<h3>From APM-Ready to Product Leader</h3>\n<hr>\n<p><strong>Volume:</strong> 21 Parts | ~124,000 words | ~450 pages</p>\n<p><strong>Audience:</strong> Aspiring Google APM-grade Product Managers; PMs targeting senior roles at top global and Indian technology companies; founders and 0→1 builders</p>\n<p><strong>Approach:</strong> First-principles depth with India + global examples woven throughout; AI Product Management as a first-class discipline; practice exercises with worked solutions in every chapter</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Preface</h2>\n<p>This handbook exists because most PM education optimizes for breadth over depth, recency over rigor, and frameworks over judgment. The PMs who succeed at the top of the field — at Google, Meta, Stripe, and at India's most ambitious companies — operate from a different foundation. They have internalized customer empathy, structured thinking, strategic clarity, and execution discipline at a level that takes years to develop.</p>\n<p>This book is your shortcut to that depth. It will not make you a great PM by reading alone. It will give you the scaffolding — the frameworks, the examples, the practice exercises, the worked solutions — that, applied with discipline, can compress years of learning into months.</p>\n<p>Three commitments shape every chapter:</p>\n<p><strong>1. Depth over surface coverage.</strong> Where most PM books explain a framework in a paragraph, we work through it with examples, edge cases, and counterarguments. Where most books reference a teardown, we deliver a complete one. The goal is not to know <em>of</em> concepts; it is to <em>operate</em> them.</p>\n<p><strong>2. AI woven throughout, not bolted on.</strong> AI Product Management is not a chapter at the end of this book. It is a thread through every chapter, with one dedicated deep-dive (Part 11) for the discipline itself. We are in the most consequential transition in product since mobile. PMs who treat AI as an add-on will be passed by PMs who treat it as a primitive.</p>\n<p><strong>3. Indian and global examples in dialogue.</strong> This book is written for an Indian audience targeting global excellence. The examples reflect that. UPI, Aadhaar, Cred, Zerodha, Swiggy, PhonePe, Indian regulatory context, vernacular UX considerations, and Tier-2/3 design realities sit alongside Spotify, Notion, Stripe, Cursor, Perplexity, and Airbnb. The PMs who can navigate both worlds have a competitive advantage no one else in their cohort possesses.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>How to Use This Book</h2>\n<p>This book is designed to be read in three modes:</p>\n<p><strong>Mode 1: Sequential reading (8–12 weeks).</strong> Read every part in order. This is the foundational pass. It takes ~50–80 hours and builds the complete mental model.</p>\n<p><strong>Mode 2: Active practice (12 months).</strong> Use Part 20's roadmap to convert the book into a 12-month plan. Read sections aligned with your monthly theme. Apply the practice exercises in each part.</p>\n<p><strong>Mode 3: Reference (ongoing).</strong> Once you've done modes 1 and 2, the book becomes a reference. When you face a new situation — a launch, a difficult conversation, an AI product strategy decision — return to the relevant part.</p>\n<p>Every part contains:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Frameworks</strong> with worked examples, often Indian and global side by side.</li>\n<li><strong>Practice exercises</strong> at the end of each part.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked solutions</strong> to most exercises.</li>\n<li><strong>AI leverage</strong> sections showing how to use AI tools to accelerate the work.</li>\n<li><strong>References</strong> for further depth.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The honest truth about PM education: the gap between knowing and doing is enormous. Reading this book and not practicing will produce smart-sounding mediocrity. Practicing in any direction without grounding in the right frameworks will produce undirected effort. Both together are what creates great PMs.</p>\n<hr>\n<h2>Coverage Map</h2>\n<p>The book is organized in 21 parts:</p>\n<p><strong>Foundations (Parts 1–8):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 1: Product Management Fundamentals</li>\n<li>Part 2: Product Thinking &amp; First-Principles Reasoning</li>\n<li>Part 3: User, Problem &amp; Discovery Mastery</li>\n<li>Part 4: Product Strategy &amp; Vision</li>\n<li>Part 5: Ideation, Prioritization &amp; Decision Making</li>\n<li>Part 6: Execution &amp; Delivery Excellence</li>\n<li>Part 7: Product Metrics &amp; Data Thinking</li>\n<li>Part 8: Guesstimates &amp; Structured Problem Solving</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Craft &amp; Specialization (Parts 9–16):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 9: UX, Design &amp; Product Quality (PM View)</li>\n<li>Part 10: Soft Skills &amp; PM Leadership</li>\n<li>Part 11: Building AI Products (Dedicated Chapter)</li>\n<li>Part 12: Product Teardowns (Library of Deep Case Studies)</li>\n<li>Part 13: Go-to-Market, Growth &amp; Monetization</li>\n<li>Part 14: Technical Foundations for PMs</li>\n<li>Part 15: Idea Origination &amp; 0→1 Product Building</li>\n<li>Part 16: PM-Owned Special Domains</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Career &amp; Mastery (Parts 17–21):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Part 17: Interview Preparation (Google APM-grade)</li>\n<li>Part 18: Common PM Mistakes &amp; Anti-Patterns</li>\n<li>Part 19: Learning Resources &amp; Practice System</li>\n<li>Part 20: 6–12 Month Learning &amp; Practice Roadmap + APM Readiness Checklist</li>\n<li>Part 21: Addendum — Gap-Fill Topics</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n<p>-e</p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n";
